{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: review the event sequence numbers at exact clean head 54f1512e894dfe257e165528b9f8a949b40e1759, directly citing artifact c3688a9ec229e01f9277b7c1120bacd3ca2ad0d1. Do not merge.\n\nPriority 4 of 6. It carries a wire change and a ProfileVersion advance, so it wants care rather than speed, and the two heads above it are user-visible while this one is infrastructure.\n\nThe README has promised ticket numbers as the first of three rungs since it was written and nothing outside the browser had one. Every projected event now carries a `Sequence`: its position in the log, derived from the fold's per-record index rather than assigned, counted from one so the founding seed is #1. A test pins that a re-fold of the same log gives identical numbers, that nothing is #0, and that no two statements share one — and states that statement sequences are not 1..n, because they skip ratify and supersede records and a reader expecting contiguity would misread a gap as a missing event.\n\nTwo judgements to weigh rather than accept. ProfileVersion advances to workroom-fold@2: nothing semantic moved, so the letter of \"any semantic fold change must advance this\" does not obviously apply, but a resident reusing a @1 checkpoint would serve #0 for every event, and silently wrong numbers are worse than absent ones for a number people are told to say aloud. The two pinned projections are regenerated for the same reason — that guard exists to force an explicit migration decision, and this is meant to be that decision rather than a fixture refresh. If you think either call is wrong, this is the head to say so on.\n\nA correction that belongs in the review: I told Hugh twice the browser showed no sequence numbers, having checked `shortEvent` in api.ts. It did — `ticketsOf` in store.ts derived them client-side from array position. That number described one response rather than the log and would have disagreed with every other reader on a filtered or paged projection; it now reads the fold's, with the old rule as fallback.\n\nThe truncation that manufactured the habit is gone: `shortEvent` is deleted, `eventName` gives an event its number or its identifier in full, and `shortHash` survives only where a git object is shown, with a comment saying why so nobody restores the symmetry.\n\nSKILL.md separates the two names rather than banning one, and the fold's citation resolution is untouched.\n\nGates: `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, the full `go test ./internal/... ./cmd/...` suite, `npx tsc --noEmit` and 69 UI tests pass. I have not re-run the docset gates at this head; they passed at the predecessor and nothing here touches a page, which is an inference.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c3688a9ec229e01f9277b7c1120bacd3ca2ad0d1","conditions":"A verdict of approved or changes-requested against exact clean head 54f1512e894dfe257e165528b9f8a949b40e1759, resting directly on artifact c3688a9ec229e01f9277b7c1120bacd3ca2ad0d1, with body.verdict set; the judgement says whether the sequence is stable and derived rather than assigned, whether advancing ProfileVersion and regenerating the pinned projections is the right migration call, whether counting the founding seed as #1 is right, and whether any surface still shows a truncated event identifier as if it were a name. Do not merge.","head":"54f1512e894dfe257e165528b9f8a949b40e1759","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}